Here is a Java application that generates and displays all prime numbers in the range of 1 to n, where n is a specified integer.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeNumbers {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
    int n = scanner.nextInt();

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      if (isPrime(i)) {
        System.out.println(i);
      }
    }
  }

  public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
      return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
      if (num % i == 0) {
        return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

This program first asks the user to enter a number using the Scanner class. Then, it iterates over all numbers from 2 to n and calls the isPrime method to check if each number is a prime number. If a number is prime, it is printed to the console.

The isPrime method checks if a number is prime by iterating over all numbers from 2 to the square root of the number. If any of these numbers divide the input number without leaving a remainder, the number is not prime. If no such number is found, the number is prime.

You can run this program by entering a number n and the program will print all prime numbers in the range of 1 to n.

One approach would be to create separate packages or modules for your hermetic and API key-dependent tests. Here are some steps that can help you achieve this:

1. Create a separate package for hermetic tests: You can create a new Go package, let's call it `hermetic_tests`, in the same project directory as your main package, `foo`. This package will contain all the hermetic tests that do not require an API key.

2. Create a separate package for API key-dependent tests: Similarly, you can create another new Go package, let's call it `api_tests`, in the same project directory. This package will contain all the tests that require an API key.

3. Write tests in separate files: Within each package, create separate test files for each test suite. This way, you can run only the tests that you want to run, depending on your requirements.

4. Use the `test` flag to specify which tests to run: You can use the `go test` command with the `-test.run` flag to specify which tests to run. For example, to run only the hermetic tests, you can use the following command:

```
go test -test.run=^TestHermetic$
```

5. Use environment variables to control the tests: You can use environment variables to control which tests to run. For example, you can set an environment variable `TEST_API_KEY` to a non-empty value if you want to run the API key-dependent tests. You can then use this environment variable to determine whether to run the API key-dependent tests or not.

Here is an example of how you can write your tests in two separate packages:

```go
// hermetic_tests/test_hermetic.go
package hermetic_tests

import (
	"testing"
)

func TestHermetic1(t *testing.T) {
	// Test 1 for hermetic tests
}

func TestHermetic2(t *testing.T) {
	// Test 2 for hermetic tests
}

// hermetic_tests/test_api.go
package api_tests

import (
	"testing"
	"os"
)

func TestAPI1(t *testing.T) {
	// Test 1 for API key-dependent tests
	if os.Getenv("TEST_API_KEY") == "" {
		t.Skip("API key-dependent tests are not enabled")
	}
}

func TestAPI2(t *testing.T) {
	// Test 2 for API key-dependent tests
	if os.Getenv("TEST_API_KEY") == "" {
		t.Skip("API key-dependent tests are not enabled")
	}
}
```

You can then run the hermetic tests using the following command:

```bash
go test -test.run=^TestHermetic$ -p hermetic_tests
```

And you can run the API key-dependent tests using the following command:

```bash
TEST_API_KEY=true go test -test.run=^TestAPI$ -p api_tests
```

This way, you can keep your hermetic tests and API key-dependent tests separate and only run the tests that you need.

You can use JDBC to fetch the result from the MySQL database. Here is an example of how you can do it:

1. Connect to the MySQL database:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class DatabaseConnectionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password)) {
            System.out.println("Connected to the database!");
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Connection failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

2. Execute the query and fetch the result:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class FetchCountExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password)) {
            String query = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employeedetails";
            try (PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement(query)) {
                try (ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery()) {
                    if (resultSet.next()) {
                        int count = resultSet.getInt(1);
                        System.out.println("Count of occurences: " + count);
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Fetching count failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

3. Use the result in your Java program:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class UseCountExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        int count = fetchCount(url, username, password);

        System.out.println("Count of occurences: " + count);
    }

    public static int fetchCount(String url, String username, String password) {
        int count = 0;
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password)) {
            String query = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employeedetails";
            try (PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement(query)) {
                try (ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery()) {
                    if (resultSet.next()) {
                        count = resultSet.getInt(1);
                    }
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Fetching count failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `your_database`, `your_username`, and `your_password` with your actual database name, username, and password.

Here is a simple, interactive user registration interface using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Note that the code requires a web server to run.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>User Registration</title>
    <style>
        body {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        form {
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            align-items: center;
            width: 300px;
            padding: 20px;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }

        input[type="text"],
        input[type="email"],
        input[type="tel"] {
            width: 200px;
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 10px 0;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }

        input[type="submit"] {
            width: 200px;
            padding: 10px;
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }

        input[type="submit"]:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <form id="registrationForm">
        <h2>User Registration</h2>
        <label for="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" required>

        <label for="email">Email Address:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" required>

        <label for="phone">Contact Number:</label>
        <input type="tel" id="phone" required>

        <input type="submit" value="Register">
    </form>
    <script>
        const registrationForm = document.getElementById('registrationForm');

        registrationForm.addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            const name = document.getElementById('name').value;
            const email = document.getElementById('email').value;
            const phone = document.getElementById('phone').value;

            // You would submit this data to a server here
            console.log('Name:', name);
            console.log('Email:', email);
            console.log('Phone:', phone);

            // Reset the form after submission
            registrationForm.reset();
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a form with input fields for the user's name, email address, and contact number. When the user submits the form, the values are logged to the console (which you would replace with a real submission to a server in practice).

The form is styled to have a clean, user-friendly design, with a centered layout and rounded borders.
